     "content": "You are comparing two drafts written from the same brief. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; judge only the text below.\n\nBrief: Write the docs-site page for the `term-weave` skill with the sections What it does, When to use it, How it works, and What you get.\n\n<<<DRAFT A\n# term-weave\n\n## What it does\n\n`term-weave` drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a folder you supply.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n\n## How it works\n\nRun the skill manually with `/term-weave <source-folder>`.\n\nIllustrative example, with documents in `./project-docs`:\n\n```text\n/term-weave ./project-docs\n```\n\nIt reads Markdown and plain-text documents and ignores other file types. If the folder is missing or contains no readable supported documents, it stops and explains the problem without drafting.\n\nEvery drafted definition links to the passage that supports it. The skill does not invent definitions: terms without a supporting explanation appear under **Unresolved terms**.\n\nWhen sources give conflicting definitions, it preserves both in one entry, links each source, and marks the entry **Conflict**.\n\n## What you get\n\nThe skill writes `GLOSSARY.md` in your working directory. It sorts defined entries alphabetically and places **Unresolved terms** after them. It never changes your source documents.\nDRAFT A>>>\n\n<<<DRAFT B\n## What it does\n\n`term-weave` drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a supplied folder. Every drafted definition links to the passage that supports it.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n\n## How it works\n\nRun the skill manually:\n\n```text\n/term-weave <source-folder>\n```\n\nIt reads Markdown and plain-text documents and ignores other file types.\n\nIt does not invent definitions. Terms without 